\section{An instruction set is not a file format}

The machine that runs a Scheme procedure need not execute the exact bytes in which that procedure is shipped. Samuel Yvon and Marc Feeley's \emph{A Small Scheme VM, Compiler, and REPL in 4K} makes this distinction concrete. Ribbit's executable contains an encoded program that is decoded to linked structures. The machine uses three-field objects called ribs for stack, code, and composite Scheme data; a data rib's third field can distinguish kinds of data, whereas an instruction rib generally uses that field to point to the next instruction. The machine's six basic operations cover literal constants, variable loads and stores, branches, and tail and non-tail calls. The tail transfer does not retain a pointer to an instruction after the call.

David H.\ Bartley and John C.\ Jensen's \emph{The Implementation of PC Scheme} makes a different choice: a register-based virtual machine with threaded dispatch and operand encodings specialized to the instruction. Naming inputs explicitly can reduce the pushes and pops required by a stack instruction set, at the cost of more operand-decoding machinery. The tradeoff is not new to software VMs. Diehl, Hartel, and Sestoft's survey \emph{Abstract Machines for Programming Language Implementation} describes the Scheme-81 VLSI evaluator, while Yvon and Feeley point to Scheme-79's linked S-code as a historical parallel to their representation. A hardware evaluator and a portable software VM can both organize control as a walk through instructions without sharing an implementation or a resource budget.

\section{Worked encoding: keep the join}

Consider the instruction graph for a body that selects a delta and tail-calls \texttt{step}. Suppress argument checks and show only the control structure:

\begin{verbatim}
entry:  get flag; if true-arm else false-arm
true-arm:   const 1;   next = suffix
false-arm:  const -1;  next = suffix
suffix: jump step
\end{verbatim}

A backward decoder can first construct the terminal jump, then link the false arm to it. To construct the true arm, start from that same jump node rather than emitting a second jump. Finish by making the branch point to the two heads and prepending the load of \texttt{flag}. At entry to the shared suffix, both paths have one delta in the same stack position. Check the decoded graph for that stack effect and for two incoming edges to the same suffix. Test both flag values, including a truthy non-boolean. If the decoder accidentally links the true arm to the false arm's head instead of its suffix, it pushes two constants and changes the calling state.

L\'eonard Oest O'Leary, Mathis Laroche, and Marc Feeley's \emph{A R4RS Compliant REPL in 7 KB} describe just such a distinction between execution and decoding instructions. The RIBN encoding builds linked sequences backwards using a decoding stack. Its \texttt{SHARE} operation takes an existing tail as the start of a new sequence; the decoded code can retain join points instead of duplicating the remainder after every non-tail conditional. Several conditionals in succession make repeated tail duplication particularly expensive. The paper also varies encoding ranges and optionally compresses the stream with LZSS. Measure the size of both the stream and the decoder: a shorter stream need not make a smaller executable.

\section{Feature selection and honest size accounting}

In \emph{A Compact and Extensible Portable Scheme VM}, O'Leary and Feeley describe host-source annotations for specializing the VM and keeping the transitive dependencies of selected features and primitives. This removes requirements no live program path can reach, provided the analysis also accounts for what a REPL can name after startup. The compiler and decoder must agree on primitive numbers and any optional calling protocol. Test a primitive whose implementation depends on a feature not otherwise selected and run the resulting image, rather than inspecting only whether the requested primitive's name occurs in generated source.

Report executable size separately from peak decoding storage and live execution heap. The 4K paper's small Scheme system and the later roughly 7 KB R4RS REPL cover different language and library requirements; those sizes are not claims about peak live memory. A port to another host can preserve decoded semantics while shifting the relative costs of dispatch, allocation, and compression.

