\section{Build a Primitive Table Before Encoding Operands}

Feature selection and operand numbering interact when the encoded program uses compact numeric indices. A small, illustrative toolchain can share a manifest between its host builder and its image encoder; the scheme below is not a reconstruction of Ribbit's format. Give each primitive a stable logical name and a fixed signature, and give each feature a list of prerequisite features. The inputs are the primitive names available to the program, including any promised interactive bindings, and the manifest. On success the tool returns the included feature set and a dense mapping from logical primitive name to image-local index. The runtime dispatch table must be built from the same mapping.

\begin{verbatim}
plan(requested, manifest):
  names = sort(unique(requested))
  pending = empty stack; keep = empty set
  for name in names:
    require manifest has primitive name
    push manifest.feature(name) onto pending
  while pending is not empty:
    f = pop pending
    if f in keep: continue
    require manifest has feature f
    # Mark before descent; prerequisites may cycle.
    add f to keep
    push each manifest.requires(f) onto pending
  # Table order cannot depend on feature traversal.
  index = number names consecutively from zero
  return (keep, index)
\end{verbatim}

Suppose \texttt{add} requires feature \texttt{math}, \texttt{display} requires \texttt{print}, and \texttt{print} requires \texttt{strings}. Request \texttt{display} once and \texttt{add} twice. The plan keeps three features: \texttt{math}, \texttt{print}, and \texttt{strings}. The table is \verb|[add, display]|. In a test image, operand \(0\) with arguments \(2,5\) returns \(7\); operand \(1\) on that result appends the character \texttt{7} to the output. Reordering the input requests leaves both outputs unchanged. Adding an interactive \texttt{car} binding shifts \texttt{display}'s index, so mixing the old image with the new table is invalid unless the loader has enough information to remap the old indices.

Every visited feature is reachable from a requested primitive, and every outgoing prerequisite of a visited feature is eventually visited. Sorting the primitive names adds a comparison cost; the closure walk is otherwise linear in its visited vertices and edges. To catch encoder/runtime drift, compare the format version and a manifest identity derived from the ordered names and signatures before using any numeric operand.
